🐱
"Cat Scat!"/en
=== start/en
¶A cat scatting, furrrever, never the same way twice.

The whole solo is improvised: nothing here is a written melody. Every chorus,
the cat picks fresh notes and the phrase is rebuilt, so what you hear now
will not come back.

It sings in five tracks that take turns rather than sound together — quarter
notes, then eighths, then triplets, then a half, then one long note — because
a cat has one voice, and two tracks sounding at once would give it two. Each
track rests through everybody else's bars, which is what keeps it a solo.

The voice is the tuned cat, \🔈.😺\: one meow, pitched, so a degree picks a
note and the cat actually sings the tune instead of saying twelve different
things. The scale is the blues, which has no wrong notes — and it has to be,
because every note here is picked at random, so any order has to sound good.

It is slow for a scat. A meow takes about eight tenths of a second to say,
and at a quicker tempo the quarter notes cut the cat off halfway through the
word.¶

beat: Beat()
count: beat.count

¶A note, or now and then nothing: about one in seven falls silent. That is
enough air for the cat to close its mouth here and there without thinning the
solo out — and since the rests land wherever chance puts them, no two choruses
breathe in the same place.¶
ƒ maybe(note•#)•#|ø (Random() > 0.15) ? note ø

¶One note, somewhere in the cat's comfortable octave. Degree 1 is the note the
phrase comes home to, and 8 is a blues octave and a bit above it — far enough
to sound like singing, close enough that the meow still sounds like a meow
rather than a stretched one.¶
ƒ voice()•#|ø maybe(Random(1 8))

¶Everything the cat sings in one chorus. Each part is as long as all the
others in beats — thirty-two — so they stay in step however differently they
subdivide.¶
•Chorus(calls•[#|ø] runs•[#|ø] swing•[#|ø] holds•[#|ø] tail•[#|ø])

¶Quarter notes, bars one and two: three notes, a breath, then the same three
answered with a new ending. Call and response is the bones of a scat phrase,
and repeating a figure before bending it is what makes it sound composed
rather than random.¶
ƒ calls()•[#|ø] (
	a: voice()
	b: voice()
	c: voice()
	(32 → []).translate(
		ƒ(i•#)
			(i = 1) ? a
			(i = 2) ? b
			(i = 3) ? c
			(i = 5) ? a
			(i = 6) ? b
			(i = 7) ? voice()
			ø
	)
)

¶Eighth notes, bars three and four: a run that climbs the scale one step at a
time from wherever it starts, wrapping back to the bottom at the top of the
octave. Sixteen eighths over eight degrees means the run climbs the whole way
twice, which is what makes it read as a run rather than a tune.

These are the shortest notes in the piece, and the one place the cat gets
clipped: two eighths in a beat is faster than a meow can finish, which is what
a fast scat syllable sounds like anyway.¶
ƒ runs()•[#|ø] (
	home: Random(1 8)
	(64 → []).translate(
		ƒ(i•#)
			~((17‥32) ∋ i) ? ø
			maybe((((home + (i - 17)) - 1) % 8) + 1)
	)
)

¶Triplets, bars five and six: a three-note cell turning over and over. Three
in the space of two is written as exactly that — 2beats ÷ 3 — which says out
loud what a triplet is.¶
ƒ swing()•[#|ø] (
	cell: [voice() voice() voice()]
	(48 → []).translate(
		ƒ(i•#)
			~((25‥36) ∋ i) ? ø
			cell[(((i - 25) % 3) + 1)]
	)
)

¶Half notes, bar seven: the cat slows down and lands on two long notes. A held
note keeps the mouth open the whole time it sounds, which is what holding a
note looks like.¶
ƒ holds()•[#|ø]
	(16 → []).translate(
		ƒ(i•#)
			(i = 13) ? voice()
			(i = 14) ? voice()
			ø
	)

¶A whole note, bar eight: degree 1, held. Coming home to the first note of the
scale is what makes a phrase sound finished rather than merely stopped.¶
ƒ tail()•[#|ø] (8 → []).translate(ƒ(i•#) (i = 8) ? 1 ø)

ƒ improvise()•Chorus Chorus(calls() runs() swing() holds() tail())

¶A new chorus every thirty-two beats. The old one is kept between boundaries,
so the solo holds still long enough to be heard; changing it exactly on the
boundary means the player splices it in on the beat rather than cutting the
cat off mid-note.¶
chorus•Chorus: improvise() … ∆ beat …
	((count % 32) = 0) ? improvise() chorus

¶The cat sways to the beat. It leans the other way on every one, so the
rocking is the pulse rather than a decoration on top of it.¶
lean: ((count % 2) = 0) ? -1 1

¶Whether the cat is making a sound right now. @Beat hands back one @Part for
each track, and a part stays ⊤ through every beat a held note sustains, so
this is ⊤ whenever anything is audible.¶
singing: beat.parts.translate(ƒ(part•Part) part.sounding).has(⊤)

¶Its mouth: open exactly when a note is sounding, shut when one isn't. Nothing
here counts beats — it asks the music what it is doing, so the face can never
drift out of time with what you hear. The scattered rests are what give it
somewhere to close, which is why about one note in seven falls silent.¶
mouth: singing ? '😺' '🐱'

🎭(
	[
		💬(
			mouth
			5m
			place: Place((lean · 0.7) · 1m -0.6m)
			rotation: (lean · 7) · 1°
			duration: 0.22s
		)
		💬('Cat Scat!' 1.4m place: Place(-2.6m 3.6m))
		🎼(
			[
				🎶(chorus.calls instrument: 🔈.😺 beat: 1beats)
				🎶(chorus.runs instrument: 🔈.😺 beat: 0.5beats)
				🎶(chorus.swing instrument: 🔈.😺 beat: 2beats ÷ 3)
				🎶(chorus.holds instrument: 🔈.😺 beat: 2beats)
				🎶(chorus.tail instrument: 🔈.😺 beat: 4beats)
			]
			tempo: 76beats/min
			scale: 🎼.blues
			name: 'scat'
		)
	]
	background: Color(16% 14 30°)
	color: Color(96% 0 0°)
)
